==================================================================
  SETUP COMPLETE. Now open TWO psql terminals and follow the
  >>> Terminal A <<< and >>> Terminal B <<< labels below.
==================================================================

==================================================================
  DEMO 1: READ COMMITTED — non-repeatable read.
  Terminal A reads the fine amount. Terminal B updates and commits.
  Terminal A re-reads and SEES the new value.
==================================================================

>>> Terminal A <<<  (begin a READ COMMITTED transaction)
    BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
    SELECT fine_id, amount FROM fine WHERE fine_id = (SELECT f.fine_id FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1);
    -- expected: amount = 1500.00

>>> Terminal B <<<  (update the fine and commit)
    UPDATE fine SET amount = 2000.00
     WHERE fine_id = (SELECT f.fine_id FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1);
    COMMIT;

>>> Terminal A <<<  (re-read the SAME row in the SAME transaction)
    SELECT fine_id, amount FROM fine WHERE fine_id = (SELECT f.fine_id FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1);
    -- expected: amount = 2000.00   (NON-REPEATABLE READ — the value changed)
    COMMIT;

== [Single-session value-change check (illustrative)] ==
BEGIN
 fine_id | amount_first_read 
---------+-------------------
   25287 |           1500.00
(1 row)

UPDATE 1
COMMIT
BEGIN
 fine_id | amount_second_read 
---------+--------------------
   25287 |            2000.00
(1 row)

COMMIT
UPDATE 1

[paste real output here]   <-- DEMO 1 two-terminal transcript (Terminal A re-read shows amount changed)

==================================================================
  DEMO 2: REPEATABLE READ — phantom insert is hidden.
  Terminal A counts active loans. Terminal B inserts one and commits.
  Terminal A re-runs the SAME query and DOES NOT see the new row.
==================================================================

>>> Terminal A <<<  (begin a REPEATABLE READ transaction)
    BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
    SELECT COUNT(*) AS active_loans FROM loan WHERE member_id = 9301 AND return_date IS NULL;
    -- expected: 0   (the only existing loan is returned)

>>> Terminal B <<<  (insert a new active loan and commit)
    BEGIN;
    INSERT INTO loan (copy_id, member_id, loan_date, due_date)
    VALUES (9302, 9301, NOW(), NOW() + INTERVAL '14 days');
    UPDATE copy SET status = 'on_loan' WHERE copy_id = 9302;
    COMMIT;

>>> Terminal A <<<  (re-run the SAME query in the SAME transaction)
    SELECT COUNT(*) AS active_loans FROM loan WHERE member_id = 9301 AND return_date IS NULL;
    -- expected: STILL 0  (snapshot isolation — phantom not visible)
    COMMIT;

>>> Terminal A <<<  (after COMMIT, a fresh query sees the new row)
    SELECT COUNT(*) AS active_loans_now FROM loan WHERE member_id = 9301 AND return_date IS NULL;
    -- expected: 1   (new snapshot includes Terminal B's insert)

[paste real output here]   <-- DEMO 2 two-terminal transcript (Terminal A still sees 0 then 1 after COMMIT)

==================================================================
  DEMO 3: SERIALIZABLE — SSI aborts one of the two writers.
  Both transactions read the same fine row, then both try to UPDATE it.
  The first COMMIT succeeds; the second COMMIT fails with a
  serialization failure.
==================================================================

>>> Terminal A <<<  (begin SERIALIZABLE, read the fine amount)
    BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
    SELECT f.fine_id, f.amount FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1;

>>> Terminal B <<<  (begin SERIALIZABLE, read the SAME fine amount)
    BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
    SELECT f.fine_id, f.amount FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1;

>>> Terminal A <<<  (UPDATE the fine and COMMIT — succeeds)
    UPDATE fine SET amount = amount + 100
     WHERE fine_id = (SELECT f.fine_id FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1);
    COMMIT;

>>> Terminal B <<<  (UPDATE the same fine; the statement blocks, then succeeds; COMMIT FAILS)
    UPDATE fine SET amount = amount + 200
     WHERE fine_id = (SELECT f.fine_id FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1);
    COMMIT;
    -- expected: ERROR: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
    --            HINT: The transaction might succeed if retried.

>>> Terminal B <<<  (ROLLBACK the failed transaction and verify the final value)
    ROLLBACK;
    SELECT f.fine_id, f.amount FROM fine f JOIN loan l ON l.loan_id=f.loan_id WHERE l.member_id=9301 LIMIT 1;
    -- expected: amount = 1600.00   (only Terminal A's +100 was applied)

[paste real output here]   <-- DEMO 3 two-terminal transcript (Terminal B COMMIT fails with serialization error)

==================================================================
  SUMMARY
  - READ COMMITTED: each statement sees a fresh snapshot, so
    non-repeatable reads AND phantoms are visible.
  - REPEATABLE READ: the transaction sees a stable snapshot taken
    at its first read; phantoms are hidden (snapshot isolation).
  - SERIALIZABLE: PostgreSQL uses SSI to detect read/write
    dependencies; one of two conflicting transactions is aborted
    at COMMIT time with a serialization failure.
==================================================================

